Make the site show what exists, and align the README with it

Checking the README's new web-application section against the site's own
web and MCP tabs found the site wrong in three places, all of them the
kind a developer discovers by trying.

The Python example imported a client that does not exist. There is no
`from asmdb import Client` taking a url and a token; `clients/python`
exposes `Asmdb(database, table, exe)`, which spawns the local binary and
talks to its REPL. Anyone following the site would fail on the first
line. It now shows the REST call, which needs no library at all, and the
caption says plainly what the shipped clients actually do.

`npx asmdb-mcp --db ./memory` fails twice over: the package is not
published to npm, and there is no `--db` flag - the server reads
ASMDB_DB, ASMDB_DIR and ASMDB_EXE from the environment.

The security paragraph said the database environment "is not reachable
from the public internet". The container is not, but the API is: it
answers on the gateway, and a probe with no credential returns 401
rather than nothing. On a paragraph whose whole job is to explain why
the token must not reach a browser, implying the database is off the
internet invites exactly the wrong conclusion. It now says the token is
the only thing between a caller and the data.

The seven MCP tool names were correct, and so was everything about the
record shape, the string-encoded integers and the length limits - which
is where the site was right and my first draft of the README was wrong.
The README gains the site's warning that find and range are full scans.

